president Trump's early-morning tweet  canceling the meeting with the Taliban  surprised many including the Afghan  government but it has actually welcomed  the decision the main goal of our peace  roadmap is is a ceasefire and a direct  negotiation with the Taliban so these  two components are very important  the Taliban refuses to talk directly  with the Afghan government which it  regards as illegitimate and corrupt but  it did agree to negotiate with the u.s.  whose forces invaded Afghanistan 18  years ago after the 9/11 attacks on  America  there have been nine rounds of talk  since October last year but during that  time Taliban fighters have carried out  numerous attacks and suicide bombings  and America has also continued its air  campaign which has hit the Taliban but  this year has also caused the biggest  number of civilian casualties the latest  Taliban bombing was in Kabul last  Thursday in which 12 people were killed  including an American soldier two days  later the US president in a tweet  ditched what he said was going to be a  secret meeting at Camp David  even as the Taliban was claiming  responsibility for Thursday's attack  zelma Khalilzad the u.s. chief  negotiator was on his way to Qatar for  further talks that were later described  as positive but the Afghan government  said the talks gave the Taliban  recognition the Taliban has responded  angrily to trump canceling the talks in  a statement released on its website they  said the agreement was finalized on  Saturday both sides agree to all points  they talked about intra Afghan talks to  begin on September the 23rd but warned  that their struggle will continue and  the US would suffer more the US has been  criticized for offering the Taliban too  much and the raising fears that the  country could descend into civil war  once the bulk of American forces  withdraw if there is no agreement  between the Taliban in the United States  the government about the travel of the  foreign foreign forces from Afghanistan  that will be the very first situation  not only for Afghanistan that will be  the very worst situation for the region  in the international community and that  is something that troubles many Afghans  we know the Taliban is not trustworthy  if they do whatever they want and they  misuse Islamic laws in a terrifying way  this country won't be better with them  and the government we are afraid if the  Taliban come out not only will women's  rights be violated but also all human  rights all sides appear to agree that  militarily there can be no winner in  this conflict but also achieving a  lasting peace is not going to be easy  either  where this leaves the peace process is  anyone's guess it depends largely in  what the Taliban chooses to do next  either escalate the violence or return  to the negotiating table in a different  frame of mind Afghanistan waits to see  which one it will be Tony Berta Lee al  Jazeera  Kabul
 

